    <string name="nc_help">Help</string>
    <string name="nc_help_ok">Got it</string>
    <string name="nc_help_cpu">Each card is one CPU cluster: cores that share a clock. The big number is the current frequency, the bar shows it against the cluster\'s hardware maximum, and Minimum / Maximum are the limits the governor may use right now.\n\nRead from /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpuN/cpufreq (scaling_cur_freq, scaling_min_freq, scaling_max_freq, scaling_governor). Core names come from the CPU part in /proc/cpuinfo. The temperature is from the thermal zone NextCore picked when it scanned the device.</string>
    <string name="nc_help_gpu">The GPU\'s current clock and load. The ring is the load in percent; the chip shows the highest clock the driver allows.\n\nRead from the GPU node NextCore found during its device scan (for example /sys/class/kgsl/kgsl-3d0 on Adreno, or the Mali / GED nodes on MediaTek). Some kernels do not report load, so it can show --.</string>
    <string name="nc_help_memory">RAM is the memory in use out of the total. Free is what apps can still get without the system closing anything (MemAvailable). Swap / ZRAM is compressed memory inside RAM that Android uses as overflow.\n\nRead from /proc/meminfo, which needs no root.</string>
    <string name="nc_help_mem_breakdown">Cached: files kept in RAM so they open faster. The system frees it as soon as apps need the memory.\n\nBuffers: short-lived storage for disk reads and writes.\n\nActive: memory used recently, which the system tries to keep.\n\nInactive: memory not touched for a while, the first to be reclaimed.\n\nSlab: memory the kernel uses for its own data.\n\nAll from /proc/meminfo. Each bar is a share of total RAM.</string>
    <string name="nc_help_battery">Level, status, health and temperature come from the battery driver in /sys/class/power_supply/battery.\n\nThe chart is the battery current over the last samples. On most kernels it is positive while charging and negative while discharging; a few report the opposite sign. Watts are current times voltage. Charge cycles and design capacity only show when the kernel reports them.</string>
    <string name="nc_help_display">Resolution, size and density come from Android\'s display API. The refresh rate is the one in use right now, and max is the highest mode the panel supports. The HDR chips list the formats the display reports.</string>
    <string name="nc_help_tweaks_perf">Lite mode keeps the performance profile from pushing the CPU and GPU to their highest clocks, so games stay smooth for longer with less heat.\n\nFPSGO settings (MediaTek only) tune MediaTek\'s built-in frame pacing engine, which raises clocks when a game\'s frame rate drops.</string>
    <string name="nc_help_tweaks_additional">Game preload loads a game\'s libraries into memory when it opens, so the first scenes stutter less.\n\nMemory killer and app priority free memory and CPU time for the game by holding back background apps.\n\nDo not disturb silences notifications while a game runs. Fstrim tells the storage which blocks are free, which keeps writes fast.\n\nThe refresh rate and render engine tiles change the screen\'s Hz and the renderer Android uses to draw its UI.</string>
    <string name="nc_help_tweaks_thermal">Bypass charging powers the device straight from the charger while you play, so the battery is not charged and makes less heat. It only works where the charging driver supports it.\n\nThermalcore watches the temperature sensors and lowers clocks gradually before the device gets hot, instead of the sudden drops that system throttling causes.</string>
